.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 23, 2026
.Dt EFI 4
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm efi
.Nd UEFI runtime services device
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/efi
.Sh DESCRIPTION
On a machine booted by UEFI firmware, the kernel calls the firmware's runtime
services: the functions that stay in memory after boot, which keep the
firmware's variables (its boot entries among them) and clock.
.Pa /dev/efi ,
root's alone, reaches them; it exists only when the firmware has runtime
services.
.Pp
Its
.Xr ioctl 2
requests take a
.Vt struct efi_var_ioc :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct efi_var_ioc {
	uint16_t *name;		/* UTF-16, NUL-terminated */
	size_t	  namesize;	/* bytes, the NUL included */
	uint8_t	  vendor[16];	/* the vendor GUID, as UEFI stores it */
	uint32_t  attrib;	/* EFI_VARIABLE_* */
	uint32_t  pad;
	void	 *data;
	size_t	  datasize;	/* bytes */
};
.Ed
.Bl -tag -width EFIIOC_VAR_NEXT
.It Dv EFIIOC_VAR_GET
Reads variable
.Va name
of
.Va vendor
into
.Va data ,
setting
.Va attrib
and
.Va datasize .
If
.Va datasize
is too small, it fails with
.Er EOVERFLOW
and sets
.Va datasize
to the size needed.
.It Dv EFIIOC_VAR_NEXT
Replaces
.Va name
and
.Va vendor
with the next variable's, in the firmware's order: an empty
.Va name
starts with the first.
.Va namesize
is the buffer's size, and becomes the name's; a buffer too small fails with
.Er EOVERFLOW
and the size needed, and the end with
.Er ENOENT .
.It Dv EFIIOC_VAR_SET
Sets the variable to
.Va data
with
.Va attrib ;
an empty
.Va data
deletes it.
Only root may, through a descriptor open for writing, and by default only the
boot manager's variables
.Po
.Li Boot Ns Ar XXXX ,
.Li BootOrder ,
.Li BootNext ,
.Li Timeout ,
.Li Driver Ns Ar XXXX
and
.Li DriverOrder
of the global GUID,
.Li 8be4df61-93ca-11d2-aa0d-00e098032b8c
.Pc
and any of OxideBSD's own GUID,
.Li 916e441e-ead7-4301-9aaf-6e0ba5a85339 .
Any other fails with
.Er EPERM
unless the
.Va machdep.efi.unsafe_writes
sysctl is 1: some firmware has made machines unbootable on writes it didn't
expect, and the Secure Boot databases are variables too.
.It Dv EFIIOC_GET_TIME
The firmware's clock, as an
.Vt EFI_TIME .
.El
.Pp
The firmware runs on a page table of its own, mapped where it expects to be.
If a call faults inside the firmware, the call fails with
.Er ENXIO
and runtime services are off until the machine reboots;
.Va machdep.efi.runtime
then reads 0.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
.Bl -tag -width machdep.efi.unsafe_writes
.It Va machdep.efi.runtime
1 when runtime services can be called.
.It Va machdep.efi.vendor
The firmware's vendor.
.It Va machdep.efi.revision
The UEFI specification it follows.
.It Va machdep.efi.unsafe_writes
1 lets
.Dv EFIIOC_VAR_SET
write any variable.
.El
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er ENOENT
No such variable, or no more.
.It Bq Er EOVERFLOW
The buffer is too small: the size needed is set.
.It Bq Er EPERM
Not root, a variable outside the allow-list, or the firmware's own refusal
(a security violation).
.It Bq Er EROFS
The variable is read-only.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
The firmware is out of room for variables.
.It Bq Er ENXIO
Runtime services are off.
.It Bq Er EIO
The firmware failed.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr efivar 8 ,
.Xr sysctl 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
device first appeared in FreeBSD 11.1; OxideBSD's, with its own request
numbers, in OxideBSD 0.3.
